  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nCentral Illinois remains positioned along the edge of a strong \nupper-level ridge with a Ring of Fire pattern resulting in daily\nconvective chances in the region, mainly to our north. \nConvective outflow originating from the mesoscale convective \nsystem that tracked from the Upper Midwest into the southern \nGreat Lakes region earlier has sagged into portions of northern \nIllinois and will need to be monitored closely this afternoon \nand evening. Thunderstorm development cannot be ruled out within\na strongly unstable (>3000 J/kg MLCAPE) but weakly sheared \n(15-20 kt) airmass. The local area is largely devoid of any \nnotable synoptic scale forcing, though, thus a mesoscale forcing\nmechanism is needed. \n\nMeanwhile, temperatures continue to run well above normal under\nthe influence of the upper ridge. The 12Z ILX sounding once \nagain measured 850mb temps just over 22C which is well above the\n90th percentile (18.6C) for early September. Deep mixing under \nmostly sunny skies will allow temps to reach the upper 90s with \na few spots tagging triple digits. Heat indices will top out \nbetween 100-110F again this afternoon and early evening with \nminimal relief overnight as temps bottom out only in the mid \n70s, around 15 degrees above the seasonal norms. \n\nOver the weekend, the upper-level ridge axis retrogrades west \ntoward the central Great Plains and amplifies, while an upper- \nlevel trough digs south across Canadian Maritimes and New \nEngland setting up an Omega Block. This will transition central \nIllinois into a northwest flow regime heading into the weekend. \nAt the surface, an expansive ridge over Ontario and Quebec will \nexpand south into portions of the Midwest pushing a backdoor \ncold front into portions of central Illinois, roughly to near \nthe I-74 corridor. This front is trending a bit slower which now\nleaves most of central Illinois with highs in the mid to upper \n90s again Saturday with heat indices again in the 100-110 range.\nWith this in mind, have expanded the Extreme Heat Warning to \nthe remainder of the area Saturday.\n\nFor Sunday and Monday, high temperatures in areas affected by \nthe frontal passage are expected to drop from the 90s down into \nthe mid-to-upper 80s. Further south, elevated heat will persist,\nbut temperatures will ease slightly with highs in the low 90s. \nHeat indices Sunday and Monday should top out in the 90s \nareawide, providing brief relief from the more oppressive heat. \nAny relief in the area will be brief, however. By Tuesday and \nWednesday, the upper ridge axis is forecast to expand back \neastward bringing a resurgence of widespread 90-degree \ntemperatures across the area with triple digit heat indices \npossible again both days.",
